{ignore}
<p><code>{at 'path'}</code> answers the value an @ expression points at - the same notation a
field can carry, <code>{$name@staff}</code>, written as a tag with the path as a quoted
parameter. The part after the <code>@</code> says where to look: a data set made with
<a href="?tag/data">{data}</a>, an array of the page, a <code>_data/</code> file, a stored
sequence, or an enclosing level by name.</p>

<p>The part before the <code>@</code> is a path through that data, its steps separated by
dots. A plain step is a field name - <code>owner.phone@shop</code> descends into the
<code>owner</code> object. A step <code>id='b2'</code> picks, among the rows at that point, the
one whose <code>id</code> field is <code>b2</code>, so <code>{at "id='b2'.name@staff"}</code> is
the name of the row with that id. Steps can be chained as deep as the data goes:
<code>{at "country.id='f0_162'.province.id='f0_17460'.name@mondial"}</code>. A name that
several rows have answers the first one.</p>

<p>The tag asserts its path: under the strict syntax check a path that reaches nothing - or an
empty value - is an error naming the path. The field form (<code>{$name@staff}</code>) is
searched more leniently, which is what <code>optional</code> lookups rest on. Use the
<code>lookup</code> pipe to find one row by a key for every row of a loop.</p>
{/ignore}
